## Findings

### High — The candidate loop can ignore protected-checkpoint and restoration failures

`prove_media_candidate` is invoked as the condition of `until`. In Bash, `errexit` is ignored throughout a function whose status is being tested by `until`, so its unguarded `checkpoint` calls do not fail closed. A PID/socket/generation/peer checkpoint can fail and execution can still reach the final successful assignment, allowing host launch. The same applies to unguarded checkpoints inside `restore_candidate_if_needed`.

The loop also treats every failed restoration as an ordinary rejected candidate. If the active identity changes, `restore_candidate_if_needed` returns nonzero, but `prove_media_candidate` subsequently prints and returns `1`; the `until` loop then invites selection of another source and can eventually continue without proving the rejected candidate was restored. Make every critical checkpoint explicit (`|| return`/hard blocker), distinguish “candidate uncontrollable but restored” from “restoration not proved,” and permit retry only for the former.

### High — UI verification does not prove a single coherent music status

`assert_ui` independently searches the whole viewport for a truncated title probe, truncated artist probe, and a play/pause glyph. Those tokens can come from different UI regions, and for Pi the purported “exactly one music status” check merely counts occurrences of the first three title words. It neither identifies nor counts the music status component. This is insufficient for the acceptance claims that both hosts render the same title/artist/state and Pi has exactly one music status, especially after `/reload`. Assert the extension's complete status row/region (identity and state together) and count that status representation exactly once in Pi.
